books per GB task; later GB tasks are dispatched when more design coverage is needed. |\nrunbooks/README.md:21:| G-series | The actual game-design work: `G` tasks create/review original game-specific bibles. `GC` checkpoints validate waves, `GD` dispatchers create GB batches, and `GQ` is the final audit. | Detailed game bibles and a completed game-design manifest in `projects/<slug>/`. |\nrunbooks/README.md-22-| B-series | The runbook writers: write the next small set of implementation instructions. \u201cBounded\u201d means one B file may write no more than five R files. | Up to five R-series files per B task; extra B files are created when more areas need coverage. |\nrunbooks/README.md-23-| C-series | Catalogue checkpoints: validate the authoring catalogue, allocations, dependencies, and limits before the next writing wave. | No product work. C files are internal coordination files in `projects/<slug>/authoring-runbooks/`. |\nrunbooks/README.md-24-| D-series | Dispatchers: expand one eligible planning chapter and create the next bounded B-series writing wave when more work remains. | More B-series authoring files, not R files or product work. D files live beside B/C files in `projects/<slug>/authoring-runbooks/`. |\n--\nrunbooks/README.md-30-game or application can scale without making any one model call responsible\nrunbooks/README.md-31-for an unwieldy collection.\nrunbooks/README.md-32-\nrunbooks/README.md:33:`GC` means **game-design checkpoint**, `GD` means **game-design dispatcher**,\nrunbooks/README.md:34:and `GQ` means **game-design final audit**. `C` means **catalogue checkpoint**\nrunbooks/README.md-35-and `D` means **implementation-runbook dispatcher**. They are factory-internal\nrunbooks/README.md-36-coordination runbooks, so they are far less important to the application than\nrunbooks/README.md-37-the F/GB/G/B/R chain: they neither define the product nor build it. They exist\nrunbooks/README.md-38-only to let a very large design or implementation catalogue grow safely without\nrunbooks/README.md-39-duplicating, skipping, or overloading a GB or B writer.\nrunbooks/README.md-40-\nrunbooks/README.md:41:For games, `F005` creates a project-scoped `game-design-runbooks/` collection.\nrunbooks/README.md-42-`GD0001` selects the detailed design work from the game's actual design signals,\nrunbooks/README.md-43-then allocates `GB` writers. Each GB file writes at most five detailed `G`\nrunbooks/README.md-44-design tasks; `GC` checkpoints validate them, later `GD` files dispatch further\nrunbooks/README.md-45-waves, and `GQ` performs the final design audit. Supervisor treats this as a\nrunbooks/README.md:46:prerequisite collection: it completes G work before F006 can create\nrunbooks/README.md-47-implementation contracts. G files are not generic RPG tasks\u2014an RPG might plan\nrunbooks/README.md-48-story, world, characters, bosses, quests, and jobs; football trivia might plan\nrunbooks/README.md-49-question sourcing, categories, adjudication, scoring, fairness, and editorial\n--\nrunbooks/README.md-104-\nrunbooks/README.md-105-### How games remain genre-specific\nrunbooks/README.md-106-\nrunbooks/README.md:107:For a game, F001 first records evidence-based **game-design signals**\u2014such as\nrunbooks/README.md-108-the primary player activity, session model, content source, and creative\nrunbooks/README.md-109-intensity\u2014and proposes relevant and rejected design modules. F002 turns only\nrunbooks/README.md:110:the selected modules into a game-design bible. F005 then produces a concrete,\nrunbooks/README.md-111-machine-readable production inventory. This avoids treating a game as a fixed\nrunbooks/README.md-112-RPG template: an RPG can receive named party, world, boss, quest, and ability\nrunbooks/README.md-113-cards, while a football-trivia game receives question provenance, categories,\n--\nrunbooks/README.md-119-\nrunbooks/README.md-120-`F001`\u2013`F004` understand the brief and domains. For a game, `F005` create